.TH QFW-DEVICE-ACCESS.YAML 5 "October 2026" "QFw 0.1.0" "QFw File Formats"
.SH NAME
qfw-device-access.yaml \- bind logical QFw devices to providers and credentials
.SH DESCRIPTION
The device-access configuration is a site-owned YAML file that maps logical
QFw device IDs to provider devices, API endpoints, and credential providers.
A service entry's
.B device-id
selects one device from this file.
.PP
The
.B service.device-access-config
field in
.BR qfw-site.yaml (5)
selects the active file. This separation allows a service manifest to describe
reusable QPM implementations while each site supplies its own devices,
endpoints, and credential integration.
.PP
Only the account running a device-backed QPM needs read access. The QPM manager
passes the selected path to the QPM process. Application processes do not
receive the file or provider credentials.
.SH QPUS MAPPING
The top-level
.B qpus
mapping contains one entry for each logical device ID.
.TP
.B provider
Provider implementation used by the device, such as
.BR iqm .
.TP
.B provider-device-id
Device name expected by the provider API. The logical QFw ID remains the key
under
.BR qpus .
.TP
.B url
Provider API endpoint.
.TP
.B credential-db
Path to a file-backed credential database. A relative path is resolved from
the directory containing the device-access file.
.TP
.B credential-provider
Name of an entry in the optional top-level
.B credential-providers
mapping. Use this instead of
.B credential-db
when a site integrates another credential source.
.TP
.B aliases
Optional alternate names that may resolve to this device.
.TP
.B resource-type
QRMI resource type to open for the device, such as
.B IQMServer
or
.BR IBMQiskitRuntimeService .
Optional when the provider serves only one QRMI resource type. IBM devices
using QRMI must select
.BR IBMQiskitRuntimeService ,
.BR IBMQuantumComputeService ,
or
.BR IBMQuantumSystem .
IBM QDMI supports only
.BR IBMQiskitRuntimeService .
A device using only QDMI can omit this field. For the other IBM resource
types, set
.B libraries
to
.BR [qrmi] .
A
.B pasqal
device sets
.BR PasqalCloud ,
the only Pasqal resource type the shim runs.
.TP
.B service-crn
IBM Cloud service instance CRN for an
.B ibm
device. Both QRMI and QDMI require one. An instance can serve
several devices and many users, and a user can be assigned to several
instances. This value is the default for users whose credential entry does not
name an instance, which a file credential database does with
.BR service_crn .
Quote the value, since a CRN ends in colons that YAML would otherwise misread.
.B QFW_IBM_SERVICE_CRN
overrides this default when set in the QPM's environment. A CRN in a
reservation's credential takes precedence over both.
.TP
.B iam-endpoint
IBM Cloud IAM endpoint that exchanges an API key for a token, for an
.B ibm
device through QRMI or QDMI. Optional. The default,
.BR https://iam.cloud.ibm.com ,
serves public IBM Cloud.
.B QFW_IBM_IAM_ENDPOINT
overrides it when set in the QPM's environment.
.TP
.BR s3-endpoint ", " s3-bucket ", " s3-region
Object storage that an
.B IBMQuantumSystem
device stages results through.
.B Required
for that resource type: QRMI resolves them when the resource is opened and
fails without them, and result staging fails later regardless. The matching
.BR QFW_IBM_S3_ENDPOINT ,
.BR QFW_IBM_S3_ENDPOINT_FOR_QSAPI ,
.B QFW_IBM_S3_BUCKET
and
.B QFW_IBM_S3_REGION
override them when set in the QPM's environment. A site service inherits no
such variables, so configure the store here for one. The access key pair that
goes with it is secret and belongs in the credential database, under
.B aws_access_key_id
and
.BR aws_secret_access_key ,
never in this file. It is required too.
.TP
.B s3-endpoint-for-qsapi
Optional. The address the quantum system itself reaches the store on, when it
differs from the one the client uses.
.B QFW_IBM_S3_ENDPOINT_FOR_QSAPI
overrides it.
.TP
.B job-timeout-seconds
Seconds QFw waits for a QDMI job before requesting cancellation, including
time spent in the provider queue. Defaults to 300 seconds; a timeout supplied
with the circuit takes precedence.
.IP
For QRMI, seconds allowed before abandoning a job on an
.B IBMQuantumSystem
device, which requires the value. Optional here: it defaults to the same
timeout this driver polls for, so QRMI does not give up on a job still being
waited on.
.B QFW_IBM_JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
overrides it. The other IBM resource types treat the same QRMI variable as
optional, so QFw leaves it unset for them. On every QRMI device it is also
how long the shim waits for a job before cancelling it, 300 seconds when
unset; on a
.B pasqal
device a job without it waits as long as Pasqal Cloud queues it, or until
its reservation closes. The value is a number of seconds.
.IP
The wait covers the whole job, including time spent waiting out a provider
rate limit. A QRMI submission, status poll or result fetch that the provider
refuses with HTTP 429 is tried again after a wait that doubles from 2 to 60
seconds, with jitter, until it goes through or this timeout runs out. QRMI
passes on no
.BR Retry-After .
.TP
.B poll-interval-seconds
Seconds the shim waits before it first polls a job's status, through QRMI or
QDMI. Optional, 1 when unset. Each poll that finds the job unfinished
doubles the wait, up to 60 seconds, which is how IQM's own QDMI library
polls. A provider that limits its request rate counts every poll, so raise
this for a device whose limit is tight. A circuit that names its own poll
interval is polled at that fixed interval instead. The value is a number of
seconds.
.TP
.B max-concurrent-jobs
How many of the device's circuits the QPM lets be at the provider at once,
from submission to result. Optional. It is a whole number, at least 1, and a
service whose device sets anything else fails to start. The QPM holds the
other circuits in its queue and sends the next as one finishes. Set it for a
provider that rate-limits.
.IP
The QPM also applies its operator limit,
.BR max_inflight ,
and the smaller of the two wins. That limit is 1 unless an operator raises
it with the QPM's
.B configure_dispatch_limits
call, so by default a QPM already sends a device one job at a time. This key
keeps the device to its own limit once an operator raises the QPM's. Unset,
the device adds no limit of its own.
.TP
.B project-id
Pasqal Cloud project that jobs on a
.B pasqal
device are billed to. It is the default for users whose credential entry does
not name a project, which a file credential database does with
.BR project_id .
A project in a reservation's credential takes precedence. A job needs a
project, which a Pasqal config file under
.B PASQAL_CONFIG_ROOT
may name instead. The device itself, such as
.B EMU_FREE
or
.BR FRESNEL ,
is the
.BR provider-device-id .
.SH CREDENTIAL PROVIDERS
The optional
.B credential-providers
mapping defines named credential integrations. Supported provider types include
.BR file ,
.BR no-secret ,
and Python plugin providers. A file provider uses
.B credential-db
or
.BR path .
Its optional fields include
.BR refresh-policy ,
.BR ttl-ns ,
and
.BR ttl-seconds .
A plugin provider uses
.B module
and may select a class with
.BR class .
.SH SECURITY
The device-access file contains device endpoints, credential-provider
references, IBM service instance CRNs, and the location of the object storage
an IBM Quantum System stages results through. It holds no secrets: API keys
and object storage access keys live only in a referenced file credential
database, which must be readable only by the QPM service account. Keep both
files outside client-readable runtime directories.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
qpus:
  site-qpu:
    provider: iqm
    provider-device-id: default
    url: https://provider.example/
    credential-db: qpu-users.json
  ibm-qpu:
    provider: ibm
    provider-device-id: ibm_torino
    resource-type: IBMQiskitRuntimeService
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/api/v1
    service-crn: "crn:v1:bluemix:public:quantum-computing:us-east:a/ACCT:INST::"
    credential-db: qpu-users.json
  ibm-onprem:
    provider: ibm
    provider-device-id: ibm_system_two
    resource-type: IBMQuantumSystem
    libraries: [qrmi]
    url: https://qs.example/api/v1
    service-crn: "crn:v1:bluemix:public:quantum-computing:us-east:a/ACCT:INST::"
    s3-endpoint: https://store.example
    s3-bucket: qfw-results
    s3-region: us-east
    credential-db: qpu-users.json
  pasqal-emu-free:
    provider: pasqal
    provider-device-id: EMU_FREE
    resource-type: PasqalCloud
    url: https://apis.pasqal.cloud
    project-id: PROJECT
    libraries: [qrmi]
    credential-db: qpu-users.json
.EE
